    {
      "id": "ground-meaning",
      "type": "page-section",
      "label": "Meaning",
      "position": {
        "x": 30,
        "y": -1526
      },
      "width": 520,
      "height": 320,
      "data": {
        "title": "Meaning",
        "body": "**What is this thing?** The ground is where a graph lands when it is opened, and what you stand on inside it. The first face is the graph's outside; the ground is its inside. In a scene the ground is the picture or poster the nodes are placed on. A graph that is only a document has no ground: its first face is the page, drawn across the graph, and there is nothing to land on.\n\nEncoding: a `ground` edge from the root's `ground` port to a node.",
        "presentation": {
          "glyph": {
            "kind": "icon",
            "name": "Article"
          },
          "baseLevel": "detail"
        },
        "_placement": {
          "version": 1,
          "state": "auto-placed",
          "source": "bridge",
          "updatedAt": 1791592070375,
          "reason": "bridge-reference"
        },
        "identity": {
